      "content": "In the 1920s, pro-segregation advocates typically relied on racist and pseudoscientific arguments to justify their positions. Some common arguments include:\n\nThe \"Scientific Theory of Racial Hierarchy,\" which claimed that lighter-skinned races were superior to darker-skinned ones and that segregation was necessary for racial purity. The \"White Man's Burden\" concept, which posited that whites had a duty to maintain control and order over \"inferior\" racial groups. The idea of \"separate but equal,\" which was often used to justify segregation in public facilities, schools, and workplaces.\n\nThese arguments often relied on unproven racial theories, misinterpretations of data, and manipulations of history, and they were used to perpetuate systemic inequality and oppression.",
